course: dictionary
lesson: 13523
title: Imperceptible: too slight to notice
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Imperceptible means so small, slight or slow that you cannot see, hear or feel it. The part im- often means not, so you can think of it as not able to be noticed.
explain: «The hour hand of the clock makes an imperceptible movement each second.» Watch the clock for one second and you see nothing, yet after an hour the hand has clearly moved. The clue is that the change is too slight to notice in the moment, so imperceptible must mean that.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «The hour hand makes a movement too slight to notice each second.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Imperceptible is an adjective, so it describes a noun, like an imperceptible movement or an imperceptible change.
fact: «Imperceptible» means so small, slight or slow that it cannot be seen, heard or felt.
fact: «An imperceptible change» might be a plant growing: from minute to minute it is too slight to see, but after a week the difference is clear.
fact: Imperceptible can describe things you hear or feel, not only things you see, as in «an imperceptible nod».
fact: Look for the clue words around it, such as «each second» or «over time», to help you spot an imperceptible change.
exercise: «The change in the room's temperature was imperceptible.» What does imperceptible mean here? | Bright and shiny | Very cold and icy | *Too slight to notice | Very loud
exercise: Find and copy the word that means not able to be noticed. | *imperceptible | second | movement | hour
exercise: «Sam gave an imperceptible nod to his coach.» What does this suggest about the nod? | It was a long song | *It was tiny and hardly there | It was a big, loud wave | It was a jump into the air
test: «The plant grew by an imperceptible amount overnight.» What does imperceptible mean here? | *Too slight or slow to see | Very tall and fast | Hot to touch | Full of colour
test: A change so slight that you can only notice it after a long time is called what? | curt | luminous | *imperceptible | famished
exam: Which sentence uses imperceptible correctly? | The brass band played imperceptible loud music | The sun was imperceptible because it shone so brightly | Tom ate an imperceptible sandwich in one bite | *The drip of water was imperceptible, so the room seemed silent
